# Deploy After Editing

This codebase has **two separate deploy targets** that both pull from the
same source tree. Forgetting either one is how production ended up running
old code for three weeks.

## Target 1: Azure Automation runbook

`runbooks/universal_runbook.py` is downloaded to Azure Automation only
when you explicitly publish it.

After editing `universal_runbook.py`:

```powershell
.\deploy-runbook.ps1
```

SharePoint-hosted report scripts (`reports/`, `core/`, `config/`) do NOT
need deploying — the runbook downloads them from the SharePoint root at
runtime, and this OneDrive folder IS that SharePoint root, so saving +
letting OneDrive sync is enough.

## Target 2: Flask webapp on Azure App Service

The webapp at https://reports.achimonline.com runs out of a separate
snapshot that does NOT auto-update from SharePoint. It was deployed via
a zip bundle to the built-in Python 3.10 runtime.

After editing any of `webapp/`, `reports/`, `core/`, `config/`, `app.py`,
or `wsgi.py`:

```powershell
.\deploy.ps1
```

This script builds `app.zip` (excluding runbooks/tests/secrets) and
deploys it to `achim-sales-reports` in the `AchimReportsApp` resource
group. Wait ~2-3 minutes for the site to restart before hitting it.

## Which deploy for which file

| Change                            | Runbook | Webapp |
|-----------------------------------|:-------:|:------:|
| `runbooks/universal_runbook.py`   | **yes** | no     |
| `reports/**` (e.g. ordered/runner)| no*     | **yes**|
| `core/**`, `config/**`            | no*     | **yes**|
| `webapp/**`                       | no      | **yes**|
| `app.py`, `wsgi.py`               | no      | **yes**|

\* Technically picked up by the runbook on the next run via SharePoint
sync, but you may want to wait for OneDrive to flush — check the file's
sync status in Explorer before assuming Azure sees the new version.

## Every time

After either deploy, remind the user what you deployed and where to
look if they want to verify.
